  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1: Extent to which effective mechanisms are put in place for planning and monitoring of development results in selected government institutions
[Scale: 1= No  mechanism; 2= Mechanism  established, but not being implemented; 3= Mechanisms  established, and partially implemented; 4= Mechanism well-established, and fully implemented]

1.2: Extent to which Multi-dimensional Poverty Index effectively influence government plans and resource allocation policies
[Scale: 1= informed national policy debate; 2= resulted in setting an agenda for change; 3= played a catalytic role that prompted a follow-up action; 4= led to a major, transformational change]

2.1: Extent to which Development Advocate Pakistan inform and influence debates on selected development issues
[Scale: 1= Little evidence; 2= Moderate evidence; 3= Consistent evidence]

2.2: Extent to which national and sub-national youth policies / programmes are informed and benefited by UNDP&#x2019;s led analysis and recommendations on youth issues
[Scale: 1 = Not at all, (0%); 2 = to a very partial extent, (up to 20%); 3=to some extent; (21-50%); 4= to a significant extent; (51-85%); 5=to full extent (100%)]

2.3: Extent to which  evidence based analysis and best practices on human development inform public policies / UNDP programming in Pakistan
[Scale: 1= Not adequately; 2= Very partially; 3= Partially; 4= Largely; 5= Fully]

3.1: Extent to which the cross-units project(s) support have been effective 
[Scale: 1 = Not at all, (0%); 2= to a very partial extent (upto 20%); 3=to some extent (21-50%); 4= to a significant extent (51-85%); 5=to full extent (100%)]</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2013">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1:  1= No Mechanism
1.2:  1= Informed national policy debate

2.1:  1= Little evidence
2.2:  1= Not at all
2.3:  2= Very partially

3.1:  2= To a very partial extent</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1: Scale 5 = Fully (100%)
1.2: Scale 5 = Fully (100%)


2.1: Scale 4 = Largely (75% and above)
3.1: Scale 3 = Partially (51% - 75%)</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1: Fully (5 DAP publications, 4 regular and one special issue was released in 2021). 
1.2: Fully (NHDR on inequality launched in April 2021. The NHDR was widely circulated at national and sub-national levels including advocacy and dissemination sessions/webinars on the the themes of the report and provincial launched to create policy impact). 

2.1: Largely (UNDP produced research on SDG financing including Insurance Risk Financing and Nature Performance Bonds in collaboration with ECCU, and on Covid-19 Green Economic Recovery through Urban Development &amp; Resilience with CPRU. In addition, it also developed Gender Mainstreaming Strategy for SDGs project and undertook policy research and advocacy on social protection)

3.1: Partially (The project facilitated Balochistan SDGs project based on its experience with challenge cup on water recycling/conservation in hospitality sector. It also organized challenge competition on urban resilience and development in informal settlements in Karachi).</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1: Largely
1.2: Largely

2.1: Largely
3.1: Partially</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1: Largely (In 2020, three issues of Development Advocate Pakistan were published and one issue in process)
1.2: Largely (NHDR on inequality finalized and to be launched in December. Urban Platform established in Rawalpindi and pilots undertaken on water recycling and conservation, urban resilience. 

2.1: Largely (UNDP supported development of a proposal on debt swap and green sukuk. The initiatives guide government on innovative financing)

3.1: Partially (UNDP undertook bootcamps on water conservation in hospitality sector and undertook interventions on water recycling in mosques).</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1: Scale 5 = Fully (100%) 
1.2: Scale 5 = Fully (100%)

2.1: Scale 5 = Fully (100%)

3.1: Scale 5 = Largely (75% and above)</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.1: Scale 5 [3 DAP publications published in 2022 and the 4th publication is in process) 
1.2: Scale 5 [NHDR on digital transformation was initiated. The report research team was put in place, research methodology finalized, and research initiated. The report governance structure was also put in place. Urban conference was successfully organized to raise advocacy and inform policy and programing on urban issues]. 

2.1: Scale 5 [In 2022, the CPD 2023-2027 was produced with technical inputs from the project on the four pillars. The project also launched SDG project development facility and prepared proposal on Flood Response and a policy memo on Pakistan Financial situation. The project produced research on thematic areas relevant for other UNDP projects and units including those on urban resilience, gender mainstreaming, blue economy and regional connectivity and cross border trade, in addition to the 3 DAPs].

3.1: Scale 5 [In 2022, the project adopted a Social Innovation Platform approach in execution of activities, including interventions in Hushey Valley on promoting local livelihood, promoting urban resilience and development in informal settlements in Karachi with interventions around key urban resilience themes such as sanitation, women&#x2019;s livelihoods, and environment. The project also collaborated with CPRU to develop a digital dashboard and complaint addressal system to enhance efficiency of customs].</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
